  ("Proof. For each rule l → r of R, since l =_{E} r, if s ⇒ t by l → r, then s =_{E} t by" 0.1812 0.8729 0.1204 0.1400 0.1377 0.0134 0.0073 0.3490 0.0513)
  ("applying the instantiation and monotonicity rules on l =_{E} r. Applying this result" 0.1812 0.8729 0.1439 0.1586 0.1573 0.0134 0.0070 0.2660 0.0717)
  ("to each rewriting, we have s =_{E} s ↓_{R} and t =_{E} t ↓_{R} . If s ↓_{R} = t ↓_{R} , then s =_{E} t." 0.1812 0.8496 0.1596 0.1793 0.1769 0.0134 0.0064 0.3352 0.0170)
  ("If s =_{E} t, check all the six conditions for obtaining s =_{E} t: If s =_{E} t because" 0.2282 0.8729 0.1831 0.1978 0.1965 0.0134 0.0066 0.2728 0.0127)
  ("s = t ∈ E, then s ↓_{R} = t ↓_{R} . If s =_{E} t because t =_{E} s, then t ↓_{R} = s ↓_{R} implies" 0.1812 0.8729 0.1989 0.2185 0.2162 0.0134 0.0071 0.3210 0.0090)
  ("s ↓_{R} = t ↓_{R} . If s =_{E} t because s =_{E} r and r =_{E} t, then s ↓_{R} = r ↓_{R} = t ↓_{R} . If" 0.1812 0.8729 0.2185 0.2381 0.2358 0.0134 0.0077 0.3088 0.0090)
  ("s = f (..., s_{i} , ...) =_{E} f (..., t_{i} , ...) = t because s_{i} =_{E} t_{i} , then s_{i} ↓_{R} = t_{i} ↓_{R} implies" 0.1812 0.8729 0.2381 0.2578 0.2554 0.0134 0.0085 0.4226 0.0090)
  ("s ↓_{R} = t ↓_{R} . If s = s′ σ =_{E} t′ σ = t because s′ =_{E} t′ , then s′ ↓_{R} = t′ ↓_{R} implies" 0.1812 0.8729 0.2571 0.2774 0.2750 0.0134 0.0081 0.3912 0.0090)
  ("s ↓_{R} = t ↓_{R} . In all cases, we have s ↓_{R} = t ↓_{R} if s =_{E} t." 0.1812 0.6386 0.2774 0.2970 0.2947 0.0134 0.0054 0.3450 0.0090)
  ("2" 0.8583 0.8729 0.2774 0.2970 0.2970 0.0196 nil nil 0.0146)
  ("Even when a rewrite system is canonical we may still want to exercise carefully" 0.2282 0.8730 0.3100 0.3234 0.3234 0.0134 0.0049 0.3311 0.0417)
  ("our choices of rules and positions in a term to apply rules, in order to minimize the" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3296 0.3430 0.3430 0.0134 0.0059 0.2972 0.0276)
  ("amount of computational eﬀort required to ﬁnd the canonical form. We may want" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3492 0.3626 0.3626 0.0134 0.0066 0.2940 0.0632)
  ("to explore only the shortest rewriting steps. For instance, choosing a position for" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3688 0.3822 0.3822 0.0134 0.0073 0.2149 0.0170)
  ("rewriting, we may use an outermost-position-ﬁrst strategy or an innermost-position-" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3885 0.4019 0.4019 0.0134 0.0048 0.3050 0.0808)
  ("ﬁrst strategy. A careful inspection of the rules to be applied may reveal that which" 0.1812 0.8730 0.4081 0.4215 0.4215 0.0134 0.0060 0.2650 0.0330)
  ("strategy works better." 0.1812 0.3652 0.4277 0.4411 0.4411 0.0134 0.0064 0.1944 0.0676)
  ("7.2.4 The Knuth-Bendix Completion Procedure" 0.1812 0.5919 0.4807 0.4941 0.4941 0.0134 0.0064 0.3182 0.0393)
  ("In Example 7.2.8, we have seen that the term t = (i(x) ∗ x) ∗ y has two normal" 0.2282 0.8729 0.4977 0.5173 0.5150 0.0134 0.0055 0.3510 0.0175)
  ("forms, i.e., i(x) ∗ (x ∗ y) and y. The rewrite system in the example is not conﬂuent." 0.1812 0.8730 0.5173 0.5370 0.5346 0.0134 0.0060 0.3239 0.0516)
  ("Knuth and Bendix suggested a procedure which takes a set of equations and a" 0.1812 0.8730 0.5408 0.5542 0.5542 0.0134 0.0090 0.1826 0.0537)
  ("simpliﬁcation order, and each equation is oriented into rewrite rules according to" 0.1812 0.8730 0.5605 0.5739 0.5739 0.0134 0.0077 0.2323 0.1117)
  ("the simpliﬁcation order. So-called critical pairs are then computed from the rewrite" 0.1812 0.8729 0.5801 0.5935 0.5935 0.0134 0.0055 0.2955 0.0266)
  ("rules. If all the critical pairs have the same normal form, we obtain a canonical" 0.1812 0.8730 0.5997 0.6131 0.6131 0.0134 0.0081 0.2701 0.0447)
  ("rewrite system. If not, such critical pairs are added into the set of equations and" 0.1812 0.8730 0.6193 0.6327 0.6327 0.0134 0.0073 0.2314 0.0584)
  ("continue." 0.1812 0.2566 0.6389 0.6524 0.6524 0.0134 nil 0.1869 0.0755)
  ("Deﬁnition 7.2.12. Given two rewrite rules which share no variables and may diﬀer" 0.1812 0.8729 0.6712 0.6846 0.6846 0.0134 0.0054 0.3037 0.0952)
  ("only by variable names, say l_{1} → r_{1} and l_{2} → r_{2} , a critical pair of the two rules is" 0.1812 0.8729 0.6869 0.7065 0.7042 0.0134 0.0072 0.3295 0.0347)
  ("a pair of terms s = t, where s = r_{1} σ, t = l_{1} [p ↼ r_{2} ]σ, p is a non-variable position" 0.1812 0.8730 0.7104 0.7251 0.7238 0.0134 0.0072 0.3590 0.0098)
  ("of l_{1} , and σ is the mgu of l_{1} /p and l_{2} . We say l_{2} is superposed at p of l_{2} to produce" 0.1812 0.8730 0.7300 0.7447 0.7434 0.0134 0.0064 0.3282 0.0156)
  ("the critical pair and this process is called superposition." 0.1812 0.6458 0.7496 0.7630 0.7630 0.0134 0.0068 0.2099 0.0249)
  ("In Example 7.2.8, we are given three rewrite rules. If we rename the variables" 0.2282 0.8730 0.7818 0.7953 0.7953 0.0134 0.0058 0.2965 0.0175)
  ("in rule 3 as (x_{3} ∗ y_{3} ) ∗ z_{3} → x_{3} ∗ (y_{3} ∗ z_{3} ), we can compute a critical pair from rule 3" 0.1812 0.8730 0.7976 0.8172 0.8149 0.0134 0.0060 0.3807 0.0159)
  ("and rule 2, by superposing l_{2} = i(x) ∗ x at position 1 of l_{3} , that is, unifying l_{2} and" 0.1812 0.8729 0.8172 0.8369 0.8345 0.0134 0.0067 0.2817 0.0308)
  ("l_{3} /1 = x_{3} ∗y_{3} with the mgu σ = [x_{3} ↼ i(x), y_{3} ↼ x, z_{3} ↼ y] and generate the critical" 0.1812 0.8729 0.8369 0.8565 0.8541 0.0134 0.0054 0.4236 0.0126)
  ("pair (i(x) ∗ (x ∗ y), e ∗ y) from l_{3} σ = (i(x) ∗ x) ∗ y as illustrated in Example 7.2.8." 0.1812 0.8612 0.8565 0.8761 0.8737 0.0134 0.0044 0.3506 0.0329))
