  ("Theorem 7.2.18. If KnuthBendix(E, ) returns R, then R is a decision procedure" 0.1812 0.8729 0.6627 0.6823 0.6799 0.0134 0.0062 0.3229 0.0858)
  ("for E." 0.1812 0.2329 0.6861 0.6996 0.6996 0.0134 0.0068 0.3639 0.0237)
  ("Proof. (sketch) The previous theorem tells us that R is canonical. In light of The-" 0.1812 0.8729 0.7173 0.7307 0.7307 0.0134 0.0066 0.2698 0.0513)
  ("orem 7.2.11, the condition 1 is ensured by Lemma 7.2.13; the condition 2 is true" 0.1812 0.8730 0.7369 0.7503 0.7503 0.0134 0.0077 0.2586 0.0414)
  ("because every equation is either made into a rule or simpliﬁed to identity." 0.1812 0.7989 0.7565 0.7699 0.7699 0.0134 0.0064 0.2632 0.0644)
  ("2" 0.8583 0.8729 0.7526 0.7723 0.7723 0.0196 nil nil 0.0146)
  ("In conclusion, rewrite systems can be used to put terms in normal form and to" 0.2282 0.8729 0.7852 0.7986 0.7986 0.0134 0.0052 0.3255 0.0175)
  ("prove terms equal, equivalent or some transitive monotonic relations. Their appli-" 0.1812 0.8730 0.8048 0.8182 0.8182 0.0134 0.0068 0.2750 0.0452)
  ("cation represents a powerful method of mathematical reasoning, because they can" 0.1812 0.8730 0.8245 0.8379 0.8379 0.0134 0.0070 0.2520 0.0510)
  ("sometimes overcome the combinatorial explosions caused by other proof procedures," 0.1812 0.8730 0.8441 0.8575 0.8575 0.0134 0.0050 0.2884 0.0863)
  ("e.g. resolution. The desirable properties of rewrite systems are: termination and" 0.1812 0.8730 0.8637 0.8771 0.8771 0.0134 0.0076 0.2679 0.0287))
  (264
  ("257" 0.8442 0.8729 0.0728 0.0862 0.0862 0.0134 nil 0.0000 0.0287)
  ("conﬂuence. Rewrite systems can be shown to terminate with the aid of simpliﬁca-" 0.1812 0.8730 0.1243 0.1377 0.1377 0.0134 0.0067 0.2857 0.0914)
  ("tion orders. Rewrite systems can be shown to be conﬂuent by looking at critical" 0.1812 0.8730 0.1439 0.1573 0.1573 0.0134 0.0077 0.2330 0.0329)
  ("pairs formed from pairs of rules." 0.1812 0.4509 0.1635 0.1769 0.1769 0.0134 0.0064 0.3139 0.0405)
  ("7.3" 0.1812 0.2093 0.2165 0.2299 0.2299 0.0134 nil 0.2858 0.0281)
  ("Inductive Theorem Proving" 0.2312 0.4972 0.2165 0.2299 0.2299 0.0134 0.0073 0.2677 0.0903)
  ("In Example 7.2.3, we converted a set E of equations into rewrite rules, where" 0.2282 0.8730 0.2374 0.2508 0.2508 0.0134 0.0062 0.2819 0.0175)
  ("add is deﬁned using two equations: E = {add(0, y) = y, add(s(x), y) = s(add(x, y))}." 0.1812 0.8754 0.2531 0.2728 0.2704 0.0134 0.0043 0.3294 0.0299)
  ("It is easy to prove by induction on i that add(s^{i} (0), s^{j} (0)) = s^{i+j} (0) = add(s^{j} (0), s^{i} (0))." 0.1812 0.8834 0.2746 0.2900 0.2900 0.0134 0.0042 0.3818 0.0143)
  ("Since add(s^{i} (0), s^{j} (0)) = add(s^{j} (0), s^{i} (0)), it is natural to state that add(x, y) =" 0.1812 0.8729 0.2943 0.3097 0.3097 0.0134 0.0084 0.3107 0.0436)
  ("add(y, x) for all ground terms x and y. Obviously, add(x, y) = add(y, x) is not a" 0.1812 0.8729 0.3159 0.3293 0.3293 0.0134 0.0076 0.2821 0.0527)
  ("logical consequence of E and E_{ax} (the equality axioms), because E ∪ E_{ax} may have" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3316 0.3513 0.3489 0.0134 0.0060 0.2987 0.0531)
  ("models in which add(x, y) = add(y, x) does hold. It is known that some properties" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3551 0.3685 0.3685 0.0134 0.0066 0.2989 0.0580)
  ("of a ﬁrst-order formula cannot be proved by deduction or contradiction; they can be" 0.1812 0.8730 0.3748 0.3882 0.3882 0.0134 0.0053 0.2558 0.0154)
  ("proved only by induction. That is why we call these properties inductive theorems," 0.1812 0.8729 0.3944 0.4078 0.4078 0.0134 0.0063 0.2581 0.0558)
